Q: React Router 5 - pass component props to children in nested routes A: <p>Instead of relying on <code>cloneElement</code>, you can pass props directly in the <code>render</code> function.</p>
<pre><code>&lt;Route
  exact
  path=&quot;/something/:id&quot;
  render={(routeProps) =&gt; (
    &lt;RouteHandler&gt;
      &lt;ChildView channelId={routeProps.match.params.id} customProp=&quot;value&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;/RouteHandler&gt;
  )}
/&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Or If you want to avoid passing props manually in every route, you can use React Context in <code>ParentView</code>:</p>
<pre><code>const ChannelContext = React.createContext();

const ParentView = ({ channelId, children }) =&gt; (
  &lt;ChannelContext.Provider value={{ channelId }}&gt;
    {children}
  &lt;/ChannelContext.Provider&gt;
);
const ChildView = () =&gt; {
  const { channelId } = React.useContext(ChannelContext);
  return &lt;div&gt;Channel ID: {channelId}&lt;/div&gt;;
};
</code></pre>
<p>If you only need <code>channelId</code> in <code>ChildView</code>, (passing via <code>render</code>) or (Context) is clean and avoids unnecessary complexity.</p>

Q: How to use Multiple Passport Personal Access Clients for different Auth Guards A: <p>I have two guards and two user models and two oauth personal clients, and this seemed to work for me:</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>class Admin extends Authenticatable
{
    use HasApiTokens;

    // ...

    /**
     * Overrides function in HasApiTokens which just selects the passport guard driver
     * that matches the first from the providers array in auth.php
     */
    public function getProviderName(): string
    {
        return 'admins';
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>As soon as I manually defined the provider name on the classes the guards used, and called <code>createToken</code> on the specific class instances the correct client matching the <code>provider</code> column was used.</p>
<p>Note: I'm using Laravel 12</p>
